4. Signal key-transparency-auditor (third-party auditor reference implementation)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream:      https://github.com/signalapp/key-transparency-auditor
Pinned commit: 13a9126a63081c189de8422bb2e7dca770adec9f  (2026-08-18, "Update to Java 26")
License:       AGPL-3.0-only (upstream `LICENSE`)

How NYX uses it:
  * `server/kt-query/cmd/nyx-kt-auditor/` — a **Go port** (not a copy) of the
    auditor-side verification in
    `org.signal.keytransparency.audit.{Auditor, CondensedPrefixTree, CondensedLogTree}`
    plus its proof records (`NewTreeProof`, `DifferentKeyProof`, `SameKeyProof`).
    `prefix_tree.go` and `log_tree.go` keep the upstream function names and
    operation order so they can be compared line by line with the Java source;
    no upstream Java file is vendored or compiled, and nothing here re-implements
    the log-side crypto (that stays in the vendored `keytransparency` module, §3).
  * `server/kt-query/cmd/nyx-kt-auditor/testdata/katie_test_vectors.pb` — copied
    verbatim from the same repository's test resources (AGPL-3.0 `LICENSE` placed
    beside it; provenance and SHA-256 in `testdata/README.md`). Those vectors were
    generated upstream by `key-transparency-server`'s
    `cmd/generate-auditing-test-vectors`. The Go tests verify the port against
    them (10 `should_succeed` updates with per-step log roots, 5 `should_fail`
    sequences, and the tree-head signature byte-for-byte).
  * `ops/systemd/nyx-kt-auditor.service` + `nyx-kt-auditor.env.example` — unit and
    environment template for running that binary next to `nyx-kt-query`.

